1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,650
Welcome to our Parents and Carers
workshop, supporting your Autistic

2
00:00:08,650 --> 00:00:09,800
young person to flourish.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,020
The secondary years can bring a lot of
change:

4
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:16,670
different teachers, growing independence,
important friendships and a

5
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:19,060
young person who wants more say in their
own life.

6
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:22,920
Today builds on what you already know,
alongside what your young person

7
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:24,740
can tell us about themselves.

8
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:29,210
We'll explore shared language and
practical supports that fit your

9
00:00:29,210 --> 00:00:29,900
family.

10
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:31,700
Keep your workbook nearby.

11
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:35,720
Activity pauses in this walkthrough are
brief.

12
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:39,460
Pause the video whenever you need more
time to think, write or talk, and

13
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:41,100
resume when you're ready.

14
00:00:44,281 --> 00:00:49,203
I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians
of the lands on which we are learning

15
00:00:49,204 --> 00:00:54,318
today, and pay my respects to Elders past
and present. I recognise the continuing

16
00:00:54,319 --> 00:00:58,858
connections of Aboriginal and Torres
Strait Islander peoples to Country,

17
00:00:58,859 --> 00:01:03,653
culture and community. I extend that
respect to Aboriginal and Torres Strait

18
00:01:03,654 --> 00:01:06,849
Islander people taking part in this
learning today.

19
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:12,940
Our Statement of Reconciliation is in your
workbook for you to read.

20
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:16,080
For the sake of this demonstration, I'll
read it aloud.

21
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:20,040
We at Positive Partnerships value and
recognise the diversity of

22
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:23,480
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
cultures and heritage

23
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:27,680
and their proud part in Australia’s
national identity.

24
00:01:27,680 --> 00:01:31,690
We acknowledge and continue to learn about
Australia’s colonial

25
00:01:31,690 --> 00:01:36,800
past and the historic and ongoing impacts
on Australia’s First Nations people.

26
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:41,430
We believe in and strongly support the
Truth-telling of

27
00:01:41,430 --> 00:01:43,300
Australia’s colonial history

28
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:47,010
and in moving forward as a nation we must
encourage open,

29
00:01:47,010 --> 00:01:51,440
honest dialogue and build relationships
built on respect and trust.

30
00:01:54,841 --> 00:01:59,233
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

31
00:01:59,234 --> 00:02:01,759
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

32
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:06,040
Everyone is welcome to be themselves here.

33
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:08,360
You can move, stand, fidget, listen,
write,

34
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:10,840
step out or pass on an activity.

35
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:12,560
Use whatever helps you take part.

36
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:16,000
Some parents and carers here may be
Autistic

37
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,200
or otherwise neurodivergent themselves.

38
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:20,680
Your experience is welcome too.

39
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:22,120
Your workbook belongs to you,

40
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:24,040
and you decide what to share.

41
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:26,560
Please keep other families' stories
private.

42
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:29,120
When we discuss school or family
situations,

43
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:31,640
leave out identifying details.

44
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:34,720
We can learn from a situation while
respecting the people involved.

45
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:39,880
Before we settle in, take a moment to
check your surroundings:

46
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:42,560
the exits, bathroom facilities,
refreshments

47
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:44,840
and any quiet space available.

48
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:46,320
Follow the arrangements for your venue,

49
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:48,160
including smoking and vaping.

50
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:50,120
If you're watching elsewhere, make the
space

51
00:02:50,120 --> 00:02:51,800
as comfortable as you can.

52
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:54,160
Keep your phone available if you need to
be contacted

53
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:56,040
by your young person or their school,

54
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:58,720
and be considerate about sounds and calls.

55
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:01,760
Have your workbook and something to write
with nearby.

56
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:03,280
You can write questions down,

57
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:05,720
and in a live workshop you can ask

58
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:09,040
the delivery team privately or during a
break.

59
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:11,480
Attend to your own needs as we go.

60
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:16,440
Let's meet the people around us.

61
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:19,920
At your table, share your first name and
who you're here for.

62
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:23,360
You might include your young person's age
or year level,

63
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:24,920
if you'd like to.

64
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:27,280
Then share one thing you would like to
understand

65
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:29,320
by the end of the day.

66
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:31,400
Keep it brief so everyone has room,

67
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:33,840
and remember you can pass.

68
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:36,560
There's no need to share a diagnosis,
school name

69
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:40,200
or private family circumstances.

70
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:42,400
If you're watching on your own, make a
note

71
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:45,680
of your question on page four of the
workbook.

72
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:47,960
Pause here for your introductions.

73
00:03:56,200 --> 00:04:00,160
Let's come back. Write your one thing on
page four if you haven't

74
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:00,680
already.

75
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:05,240
Keep that question with you. We'll return
to it in our final session

76
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:09,470
and consider what has become clearer, as
well as what you'd still like

77
00:04:09,470 --> 00:04:10,120
to explore.

78
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:13,720
Here's the shape of our day.

79
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:17,000
We begin with your young person as a whole
person,

80
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,920
then explore sensory experience and
thinking.

81
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:24,340
After morning tea, we move into
communication, connection and daily

82
00:04:24,340 --> 00:04:24,980
capacity.

83
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:29,250
The afternoon turns towards home and
community, followed by partnership

84
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:29,980
with school.

85
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:35,860
Our final session brings the learning back
to a few small next steps.

86
00:04:35,860 --> 00:04:39,880
The timetable on the slide is a guide for
a live workshop,

87
00:04:40,040 --> 00:04:45,220
including morning tea from eleven to
eleven fifteen and lunch from twelve
fifteen to twelve forty-five.

88
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:49,040
In this recording, take breaks when you
need them.

89
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:54,210
These six sessions are connected, so keep
the same young person in mind

90
00:04:54,210 --> 00:04:54,720
throughout.

91
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:03,560
You'll hear us use identity-first
language, such as Autistic person.

92
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:04,400
Many Autistic people

93
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:08,410
prefer it because autism is part of who
they are. Other people prefer

94
00:05:08,410 --> 00:05:09,400
person on the autism

95
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:14,960
spectrum, or another description. We
respect the person's preference.

96
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:15,960
Teenagers may have

97
00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:20,020
their own strong views about the words
used to describe them. Ask what

98
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:21,000
feels right for

99
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,380
your young person, in a way that works for
them. Some are newly

100
00:05:25,380 --> 00:05:26,600
identified, and some are still

101
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:32,120
working out how they feel. Preferences can
change. There's no test on

102
00:05:32,120 --> 00:05:33,920
language today.

103
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:37,840
We can listen, learn and respect the words
people choose for themselves.

104
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:42,360
Bring your young person to mind.

105
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:46,360
What are three things you love or value
about them?

106
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:49,360
It might be their humour, an interest they
know so much about,

107
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:54,360
the way they connect with someone, or a
small moment of joy.

108
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:56,360
Write those things on page four.

109
00:05:56,360 --> 00:06:00,820
For this moment, put the list of
challenges to one side and notice who

110
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:01,360
they are.

111
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:05,360
If this is a hard season, one small moment
from the past week is enough.

112
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:07,360
Start privately,

113
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:10,360
then share one thing with your table if
you wish.

114
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:13,360
Allow four minutes for this reflection and
any sharing.

115
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:17,360
Pause the video now, then resume when you
are ready to come back.

116
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:27,560
Keep that page handy.

117
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:32,250
Those strengths and joys belong in every
conversation we have today,

118
00:06:32,250 --> 00:06:34,560
including conversations about support.

119
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:38,780
We'll come back to them at the end, when
you choose a strength to name

120
00:06:38,780 --> 00:06:39,560
to your young person.

121
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:44,760
You know your young person in ways nobody
else does.

122
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:47,920
You bring years of everyday knowledge, and
that matters here.

123
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:53,280
Today adds shared language and useful
ideas to that knowledge.

124
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:58,280
As young people get older, they also
develop expertise about themselves.

125
00:06:58,280 --> 00:07:01,800
Part of this stage is bringing their own
account alongside yours,

126
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:04,640
including when it surprises you.

127
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:08,040
We can take your observations seriously
and make room for what your

128
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:08,480
young person

129
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:10,960
wants us to understand.

130
00:07:10,960 --> 00:07:14,280
The aim is a conversation about support
that fits their life,

131
00:07:14,280 --> 00:07:16,720
with their growing agency at the centre.

132
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:21,720
We're going to hear directly from Autistic
young people.

133
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:24,600
Listen to their accounts on their own
terms.

134
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:26,520
Each speaker offers their own experience.

135
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:29,360
We don't need to compare them with your
young person

136
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:32,200
or ask one person to represent everyone.

137
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:34,320
Notice what stays with you,

138
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:37,680
and what it makes you curious to
understand more deeply.

139
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:38,520
Let's listen.

140
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:06,040
Take a quiet moment with what you've just
heard.

141
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:13,130
On page four, return to the young people's
voices. What did you notice?

142
00:10:13,130 --> 00:10:14,520
What stayed with you?

143
00:10:14,520 --> 00:10:18,770
And what would you like to understand more
deeply about your own young

144
00:10:18,770 --> 00:10:19,520
person?

145
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:21,600
Begin with a little private writing.

146
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:25,720
If you share, stay close to what the
speakers actually said.

147
00:10:25,720 --> 00:10:29,200
It's fine to feel moved, uncertain, or to
have a question without an

148
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:29,880
answer.

149
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:33,880
You can talk with someone nearby or keep
this reflection to yourself.

150
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:34,920
Pause the video now.

151
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:46,800
As we come back, keep one question in
mind.

152
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:49,760
Sensory experience, thinking and
communication

153
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:51,760
will give us ways to explore it.

154
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:54,320
A question that stays open can be useful.

155
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:56,640
We can listen carefully without assuming

156
00:10:56,640 --> 00:10:59,840
we already know what an experience means
for someone else.

157
00:11:02,120 --> 00:11:06,000
Neurodiversity means the diversity of
human brains and minds.

158
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:06,840
It includes everyone.

159
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:11,160
Neurotypical and neurodivergent describe
patterns

160
00:11:11,160 --> 00:11:14,800
within that wider picture, and autism is
one expression of it.

161
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:19,520
These words help us talk about differences
in experience and processing.

162
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:22,560
They don't tell us everything about a
particular person.

163
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:28,040
As we explore common patterns today, keep
asking how they do, or don't,

164
00:11:28,040 --> 00:11:30,200
connect with the young person you know.

165
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Each young person remains an individual.

166
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:40,160
A neurodiversity-affirming approach
recognises different ways of

167
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:41,820
knowing, doing, being and belonging.

168
00:11:41,820 --> 00:11:44,580
People think and learn in different ways.

169
00:11:44,580 --> 00:11:48,160
Everyone has strengths, and everyone can
experience challenges.

170
00:11:48,160 --> 00:11:52,460
Take the things you wrote about your young
person earlier.

171
00:11:52,460 --> 00:11:55,840
Their interests, enjoyment and way of
connecting are part of the

172
00:11:55,840 --> 00:11:57,660
picture when we think about support.

173
00:11:57,660 --> 00:12:01,860
We can take difficulties seriously while
valuing who they are.

174
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:07,130
That means asking what the surroundings,
routines and support need to

175
00:12:07,130 --> 00:12:10,900
look like so your young person can
participate comfortably and authentically.

176
00:12:10,900 --> 00:12:15,200
A hard day doesn't cancel out a strength,
and recognising a strength

177
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:17,320
doesn't make a hard day disappear.

178
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:22,100
People have many parts to their identity.

179
00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:25,200
Culture, language, gender, sexuality,
faith, disability,

180
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:27,960
age and family circumstances may overlap

181
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:30,760
and shape opportunities and barriers.

182
00:12:30,760 --> 00:12:32,920
That's the idea of intersectionality.

183
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:36,320
Adolescence can be a time of exploring who
you are.

184
00:12:36,320 --> 00:12:38,800
Autism is one part of that picture.

185
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:40,840
Belonging might be found in family,

186
00:12:40,840 --> 00:12:45,760
cultural communities, online spaces or
Autistic community.

187
00:12:45,760 --> 00:12:49,840
Some parts of identity may be private or
still taking shape.

188
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:52,640
We don't need personal disclosures here.

189
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:55,200
Support can make room for the whole young
person,

190
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:57,800
including things they haven't chosen to
share with us.

191
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:05,760
Capacity can change from minute to minute,
day to day or term to term.

192
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:09,580
Something your young person managed on a
quiet Saturday may be far

193
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:10,600
harder after a Monday

194
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:14,780
of different teachers, busy corridors and
changing classrooms.

195
00:13:14,780 --> 00:13:19,120
Assessment periods and the end of term can
add to that load.

196
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:23,680
The phrase dynamic disability helps us
keep this changing experience

197
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:24,440
in view.

198
00:13:24,440 --> 00:13:27,860
The young person is the same, but the
demands and available energy can

199
00:13:27,860 --> 00:13:28,440
differ.

200
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:33,070
When something becomes hard, ask what else
is going on and what support

201
00:13:33,070 --> 00:13:33,880
fits now.

202
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:35,800
We'll return to daily capacity later.

203
00:13:37,880 --> 00:13:40,830
Before we finish this first session, let's
look at your rights at

204
00:13:40,830 --> 00:13:42,080
school.

205
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:46,330
The Disability Discrimination Act of
nineteen ninety-two makes it unlawful to
treat

206
00:13:46,330 --> 00:13:48,000
someone unfairly

207
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:51,800
because of disability, including in
education.

208
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:56,160
The Disability Standards for Education
were introduced in two thousand and five.

209
00:13:56,160 --> 00:14:00,410
These standards set out schools'
responsibilities for access and

210
00:14:00,410 --> 00:14:00,920
participation.

211
00:14:00,920 --> 00:14:05,400
The school must consult your young person,
or you on their behalf, about

212
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:07,200
reasonable adjustments

213
00:14:07,200 --> 00:14:12,840
so they can take part on the same basis as
students without disability.

214
00:14:12,840 --> 00:14:17,090
That consultation can include your young
person directly, you as their

215
00:14:17,090 --> 00:14:20,560
parent or carer, or both.

216
00:14:20,560 --> 00:14:23,520
Here are three useful questions to keep:

217
00:14:23,520 --> 00:14:25,560
How will we be consulted?

218
00:14:25,560 --> 00:14:27,280
What adjustments are in place?

219
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:30,360
How and when will they be reviewed?

220
00:14:30,360 --> 00:14:34,160
In secondary school, this includes
adjustments for assessments and

221
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:34,720
exams.

222
00:14:34,720 --> 00:14:39,080
Ask early, especially as the senior years
get closer.

223
00:14:39,080 --> 00:14:43,330
This is general information, rather than
advice about an individual

224
00:14:43,330 --> 00:14:44,640
legal situation.

225
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:48,920
The Department of Education has
plain-language guides for families.

226
00:14:48,920 --> 00:14:52,300
We'll return to consultation, support
pathways and preparing for a

227
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:55,480
conversation with school in Session Five.

228
00:14:55,480 --> 00:14:58,130
Knowing your rights gives you a useful
starting point for that

229
00:14:58,130 --> 00:14:58,880
partnership.

230
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:04,560
Let's hold on to joy and flourishing as we
move forward.

231
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:08,440
Connection, belonging and small moments of
enjoyment matter.

232
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:10,000
Every family is different,

233
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,360
and understanding what helps can take
time.

234
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:14,920
Doing something differently is fine

235
00:15:14,920 --> 00:15:16,240
when it helps your family.

236
00:15:16,240 --> 00:15:20,200
A quieter outing, a familiar shared
activity

237
00:15:20,200 --> 00:15:22,000
or a small success can matter

238
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,360
just as much as a bigger milestone.

239
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:27,240
In our next session, we'll begin with

240
00:15:27,240 --> 00:15:29,120
how the world arrives through the senses,

241
00:15:29,120 --> 00:15:32,080
and then explore attention and thinking.

242
00:15:32,080 --> 00:15:35,320
Keep your young person's strengths
alongside your questions.

243
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:46,440
Welcome to Session Two. Keep in mind the
young person you wrote about

244
00:15:46,440 --> 00:15:47,320
earlier.

245
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:50,200
We're going to build a fuller picture of
their experience,

246
00:15:50,200 --> 00:15:55,080
beginning with sensory processing, then
thinking and internal signals.

247
00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:58,920
Each idea is something to explore
alongside what you know.

248
00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:01,960
You may recognise some patterns and not
others.

249
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:06,370
By the end, you'll have some questions to
take into everyday routines at

250
00:16:06,370 --> 00:16:06,760
home.

251
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:12,200
Before thinking, communication or
connection, there is sensory

252
00:16:12,200 --> 00:16:13,400
experience.

253
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:16,400
The world is already arriving through our
senses.

254
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:19,400
How it arrives can affect how comfortable
we feel,

255
00:16:19,400 --> 00:16:23,400
what we notice and how much energy is
available for everything else.

256
00:16:23,400 --> 00:16:24,400
Let's start there.

257
00:16:26,120 --> 00:16:28,840
What does sensory processing mean to you?

258
00:16:28,840 --> 00:16:30,560
Take a moment to put it into your own
words.

259
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:32,360
You might think about an ordinary
experience,

260
00:16:32,360 --> 00:16:34,680
such as walking into a noisy room

261
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:36,200
or putting on a warm jumper.

262
00:16:36,200 --> 00:16:39,600
Pause here if you'd like time to talk it
through.

263
00:16:48,080 --> 00:16:53,170
Sensory processing is the way the brain
receives, processes and responds

264
00:16:53,170 --> 00:16:53,920
to information from

265
00:16:53,920 --> 00:16:55,580
the senses.

266
00:16:55,580 --> 00:16:59,110
We often notice the response first, but
there is a whole process

267
00:16:59,110 --> 00:17:00,920
happening before that response

268
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:01,880
becomes visible.

269
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:08,580
Many of us learnt five senses: touch,
vision, taste, hearing and smell.

270
00:17:08,580 --> 00:17:11,120
Here we include three more.

271
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:15,600
Proprioception is our sense of the body in
space, drawing on

272
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:18,020
information from muscles and joints.

273
00:17:18,020 --> 00:17:21,280
It helps us know where our body is and how
much force we're using.

274
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:25,600
The vestibular sense relates to balance
and movement.

275
00:17:25,600 --> 00:17:30,330
Interoception is our sense of internal
body signals, such as hunger,

276
00:17:30,330 --> 00:17:32,640
thirst or needing the toilet.

277
00:17:33,620 --> 00:17:34,600
We'll return to that later.

278
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:36,860
These senses work together.

279
00:17:36,860 --> 00:17:40,730
In a busy routine, someone may be managing
several kinds of sensory

280
00:17:40,730 --> 00:17:42,020
information at once,

281
00:17:42,020 --> 00:17:44,720
including input that others barely notice.

282
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:50,360
Think about taking off a jumper because
you're getting hot.

283
00:17:50,360 --> 00:17:53,360
First, information arrives from the body.

284
00:17:53,360 --> 00:17:57,740
The brain processes it, including what
similar experiences have felt

285
00:17:57,740 --> 00:17:58,360
like before.

286
00:17:58,360 --> 00:18:02,360
Then there is a response, such as taking
the jumper off.

287
00:18:02,360 --> 00:18:05,360
This process helps us understand our
surroundings,

288
00:18:05,360 --> 00:18:08,360
keep track of our body and engage with
what's happening.

289
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:12,360
People process sensory information
differently.

290
00:18:12,360 --> 00:18:15,360
A visible reaction is just one part of
that process.

291
00:18:15,360 --> 00:18:19,850
Before judging the reaction, it helps to
ask what information may have

292
00:18:19,850 --> 00:18:20,360
arrived,

293
00:18:20,360 --> 00:18:22,360
and how it might have felt.

294
00:18:24,480 --> 00:18:26,760
We're going to watch two short clips.

295
00:18:26,760 --> 00:18:29,720
The first gives a sense of sensory
overwhelm.

296
00:18:29,720 --> 00:18:32,920
The second makes space for sensory joy.

297
00:18:32,920 --> 00:18:36,640
Both belong in our understanding of
Autistic experience.

298
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:38,600
If the sound or images feel too much,

299
00:18:38,600 --> 00:18:42,320
you're welcome to lower the volume, look
away or pause.

300
00:18:42,320 --> 00:18:44,160
Listen to the account without assuming

301
00:18:44,160 --> 00:18:47,680
every Autistic person experiences things
in the same way.

302
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:16,400
Let's leave a little space before we move
on.

303
00:21:19,960 --> 00:21:23,800
Now let's hear about sensory joy. Notice
the pleasure and richness that

304
00:21:23,800 --> 00:21:25,560
sensory experience can bring,

305
00:21:25,560 --> 00:21:28,560
alongside the overwhelm we've just
considered.

306
00:22:20,840 --> 00:22:25,080
Sensory experience includes comfort and
enjoyment too.

307
00:22:25,080 --> 00:22:29,920
A sound, movement or texture can be deeply
satisfying.

308
00:22:29,920 --> 00:22:33,560
Keep both sides in the picture when you
think about your young person:

309
00:22:33,560 --> 00:22:37,170
what asks a lot of their senses, and what
feels good or helps them

310
00:22:37,170 --> 00:22:37,640
settle.

311
00:22:40,240 --> 00:22:44,080
This framework from Winnie Dunn describes
four patterns of sensory

312
00:22:44,080 --> 00:22:44,940
response.

313
00:22:44,940 --> 00:22:48,940
Registration means some input may not be
picked up readily.

314
00:22:48,940 --> 00:22:52,340
Sensory seeking means actively looking for
more input,

315
00:22:52,340 --> 00:22:54,940
perhaps through movement, touch or sound.

316
00:22:54,940 --> 00:22:59,240
Sensory sensitivity means noticing input
strongly,

317
00:22:59,240 --> 00:23:01,940
even if the person doesn't act to change
it.

318
00:23:01,940 --> 00:23:07,440
Sensory avoiding means actively reducing
input, such as covering ears

319
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:09,740
or choosing a quieter place.

320
00:23:11,140 --> 00:23:14,240
A person may seek one kind of input and
avoid another.

321
00:23:14,240 --> 00:23:18,460
The pattern can also shift with the
setting and the load already being

322
00:23:18,460 --> 00:23:19,240
carried.

323
00:23:19,240 --> 00:23:23,150
Some families will have encountered a
sensory profile through an

324
00:23:23,150 --> 00:23:24,940
occupational therapist.

325
00:23:24,940 --> 00:23:28,740
Use this as a way to notice patterns and
ask questions.

326
00:23:28,740 --> 00:23:31,540
It doesn't give us a complete description
of a young person.

327
00:23:33,760 --> 00:23:37,850
Stimming can include pacing, tapping,
fidgeting, humming or repeating a

328
00:23:37,850 --> 00:23:38,640
favourite sound.

329
00:23:38,640 --> 00:23:43,480
It may help your young person settle,
focus, cope or express joy.

330
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:48,090
Some teenagers hide their stims at school
to fit in, and that can take

331
00:23:48,090 --> 00:23:48,920
energy.

332
00:23:48,920 --> 00:23:52,660
Notice what stimming does for them, and
make room for it.

333
00:23:52,660 --> 00:23:56,820
Fidget tools or music may help, depending
on their preferences.

334
00:23:56,820 --> 00:23:59,020
Respect privacy too.

335
00:23:59,020 --> 00:24:03,160
Some young people want space to stim
without others watching.

336
00:24:04,380 --> 00:24:08,460
If a movement causes harm, look for a
safer way to meet the same need.

337
00:24:08,460 --> 00:24:12,300
Follow your young person's lead rather
than making their expression

338
00:24:12,300 --> 00:24:13,960
another thing to monitor.

339
00:24:16,760 --> 00:24:18,960
Turn to the sensory activity on page six.

340
00:24:18,960 --> 00:24:21,220
Choose one familiar home routine:

341
00:24:21,220 --> 00:24:24,740
getting ready in the morning, dinner or
winding down at night.

342
00:24:24,740 --> 00:24:27,080
What sensory demands are involved?

343
00:24:27,080 --> 00:24:32,350
Think about sound, light, touch, movement
and the less visible body

344
00:24:32,350 --> 00:24:33,280
signals too.

345
00:24:33,280 --> 00:24:37,240
What small change could make it easier or
offer more choice?

346
00:24:37,240 --> 00:24:40,360
And what would you like to find out from
your young person?

347
00:24:40,360 --> 00:24:44,180
Write the routine, the demand and a
possible support.

348
00:24:44,340 --> 00:24:46,980
Talk through your ideas with a partner or
table if you wish.

349
00:24:46,980 --> 00:24:48,480
Pause here for the activity.

350
00:24:57,120 --> 00:24:58,920
Look back at your notes.

351
00:24:58,920 --> 00:25:02,880
Keep what you observed separate from what
you think it might mean.

352
00:25:02,880 --> 00:25:06,830
A loud bathroom or an uncomfortable
texture gives us a useful question

353
00:25:06,830 --> 00:25:08,040
to explore.

354
00:25:08,040 --> 00:25:11,600
Your young person's own communication will
help us understand

355
00:25:11,600 --> 00:25:14,200
whether the change actually helps.

356
00:25:14,200 --> 00:25:17,640
Start with one workable adjustment to the
routine.

357
00:25:17,640 --> 00:25:21,080
Your young person might find it easier to
tell you by message,

358
00:25:21,080 --> 00:25:23,960
side by side or during a drive.

359
00:25:23,960 --> 00:25:27,320
The way you ask is part of making the
conversation accessible.

360
00:25:29,880 --> 00:25:33,650
Now we move from how the world arrives to
how the mind takes it in and

361
00:25:33,650 --> 00:25:36,440
where attention goes.

362
00:25:36,440 --> 00:25:40,920
The next ideas describe patterns some
Autistic people recognise.

363
00:25:40,920 --> 00:25:44,090
Keep checking them against the person you
know, rather than assuming

364
00:25:44,090 --> 00:25:45,320
every pattern applies.

365
00:25:47,520 --> 00:25:51,170
The detail-first idea describes building
understanding from individual

366
00:25:51,170 --> 00:25:52,660
pieces towards a whole picture.

367
00:25:52,660 --> 00:25:57,500
Someone may notice a small change, an
inconsistency or a background

368
00:25:57,500 --> 00:26:00,980
detail before the overall situation
becomes clear.

369
00:26:00,980 --> 00:26:05,480
This can be connected with strengths such
as pattern recognition, deep

370
00:26:05,480 --> 00:26:09,520
memory, logical precision, honesty and
close attention.

371
00:26:09,520 --> 00:26:14,940
Think about what your young person knows
in detail, or what they notice

372
00:26:14,940 --> 00:26:16,980
that other people miss.

373
00:26:18,100 --> 00:26:21,250
These strengths are present in everyday
life. Taking in a lot of detail

374
00:26:21,250 --> 00:26:22,680
can also take effort.

375
00:26:22,680 --> 00:26:26,510
A task may be hard to begin if the larger
picture or first step is

376
00:26:26,510 --> 00:26:27,320
unclear.

377
00:26:27,320 --> 00:26:31,540
The same way of processing can bring depth
and also create demands.

378
00:26:31,540 --> 00:26:35,330
We can notice both without turning this
into a checklist that every

379
00:26:35,330 --> 00:26:36,840
young person must fit.

380
00:26:39,440 --> 00:26:42,000
Think about your own attention for a
moment.

381
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:43,740
Does it move between several things,

382
00:26:43,740 --> 00:26:45,600
or become deeply absorbed in one?

383
00:26:45,600 --> 00:26:48,840
The torch and floodlight image gives us

384
00:26:48,840 --> 00:26:51,040
two ways to picture that.

385
00:26:51,040 --> 00:26:53,880
Monotropism describes attention going
deeply

386
00:26:53,880 --> 00:26:55,640
into fewer things at a time.

387
00:26:55,640 --> 00:26:58,840
Autistic people help develop this way

388
00:26:58,840 --> 00:26:59,900
of understanding attention.

389
00:26:59,900 --> 00:27:03,780
Deep interests can offer learning, memory
and joy.

390
00:27:03,780 --> 00:27:06,840
When your young person is absorbed in a
game, a drawing,

391
00:27:06,840 --> 00:27:08,840
a favourite topic or a song,

392
00:27:08,840 --> 00:27:11,080
their attention may be deeply invested.

393
00:27:11,080 --> 00:27:15,680
Asking them to stop for dinner can involve
real effort.

394
00:27:15,680 --> 00:27:19,000
Warning, time and a helpful transition can
make a difference.

395
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:22,960
That absorbed state, sometimes called
flow,

396
00:27:22,960 --> 00:27:24,920
is also something worth protecting.

397
00:27:27,800 --> 00:27:31,560
Executive functioning is a name for
processes we use to organise and

398
00:27:31,560 --> 00:27:33,080
carry out everyday tasks.

399
00:27:33,080 --> 00:27:38,010
The slide includes planning, organisation,
time management and working

400
00:27:38,010 --> 00:27:38,760
memory.

401
00:27:38,760 --> 00:27:43,480
It also includes thinking about our
thinking, managing emotions,

402
00:27:43,480 --> 00:27:46,880
flexibility, attention, getting started
and persisting.

403
00:27:46,880 --> 00:27:50,840
We use different combinations of these
throughout the day.

404
00:27:50,840 --> 00:27:55,690
When a task is hard, identifying the
particular demand can help us find

405
00:27:55,690 --> 00:27:57,080
useful support.

406
00:27:57,800 --> 00:28:01,200
Let's look at getting started and
switching focus, because they ask

407
00:28:01,200 --> 00:28:02,920
different things of a person.

408
00:28:02,920 --> 00:28:07,360
Secondary school can add a timetable,
multiple teachers, homework and

409
00:28:07,360 --> 00:28:09,720
assessment dates to keep track of.

410
00:28:09,720 --> 00:28:14,140
A capable young person can still need
support with starting or planning.

411
00:28:14,140 --> 00:28:18,460
Executive demands and subject knowledge
are different things.

412
00:28:20,920 --> 00:28:24,440
Starting an assignment can be hard even
when your young person knows

413
00:28:24,440 --> 00:28:25,480
the topic.

414
00:28:25,480 --> 00:28:29,840
The task may contain several decisions
before any writing begins:

415
00:28:29,840 --> 00:28:31,360
what is being asked,

416
00:28:31,360 --> 00:28:33,580
what the finished work should look like

417
00:28:33,580 --> 00:28:36,640
and which step comes first.

418
00:28:36,640 --> 00:28:38,960
Work out that first step together,

419
00:28:38,960 --> 00:28:41,560
or make the parts visible.

420
00:28:41,560 --> 00:28:44,680
Perhaps the starting point is opening the
instructions and choosing one

421
00:28:44,680 --> 00:28:46,680
question to clarify.

422
00:28:46,680 --> 00:28:49,320
The subject work remains theirs.

423
00:28:49,320 --> 00:28:52,680
Our support can help make the task
understandable enough to begin.

424
00:28:55,280 --> 00:28:59,110
Switching away from a game, drawing or
deep interest can take real

425
00:28:59,110 --> 00:28:59,520
effort.

426
00:28:59,520 --> 00:29:03,690
Your young person may need time to
disengage before turning towards

427
00:29:03,690 --> 00:29:05,620
dinner or getting ready to go out.

428
00:29:05,620 --> 00:29:09,700
The interruption can feel much bigger from
inside that focus.

429
00:29:09,700 --> 00:29:13,730
A meaningful warning, time to reach an
agreed stopping point and

430
00:29:13,730 --> 00:29:16,400
clarity about what comes next may help.

431
00:29:16,400 --> 00:29:20,780
Talk together about what kind of
transition works.

432
00:29:22,100 --> 00:29:25,300
Getting started and switching are
different demands, so the useful

433
00:29:25,300 --> 00:29:26,640
support may differ too.

434
00:29:29,800 --> 00:29:33,650
This slide is reserved for a young
person's account of deep focus and

435
00:29:33,650 --> 00:29:34,880
transitions.

436
00:29:34,880 --> 00:29:39,550
The supplied workshop materials still show
a video placeholder here, so

437
00:29:39,550 --> 00:29:39,960
there isn't a

438
00:29:39,960 --> 00:29:42,360
film to play in this walkthrough.

439
00:29:42,360 --> 00:29:45,760
We'll move into the reflection using the
ideas we've just explored,

440
00:29:45,760 --> 00:29:46,840
while keeping that space

441
00:29:46,840 --> 00:29:49,240
for a first-person account when the clip
is available.

442
00:29:51,400 --> 00:29:53,360
Turn to page seven.

443
00:29:53,360 --> 00:29:56,080
What are your young person's deep
interests?

444
00:29:56,080 --> 00:29:59,000
What do you notice when they're fully
engaged?

445
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:02,680
When is it difficult to get started or
shift attention?

446
00:30:02,680 --> 00:30:04,880
Think of a time they were completely
absorbed at home.

447
00:30:04,880 --> 00:30:07,340
What were they doing?

448
00:30:07,340 --> 00:30:09,320
Choose the questions that feel useful.

449
00:30:09,320 --> 00:30:11,620
Begin with a real moment, then talk with
your table

450
00:30:11,620 --> 00:30:13,160
or write privately.

451
00:30:13,160 --> 00:30:17,700
Keep enjoyment and strengths alongside the
difficult transitions.

452
00:30:17,700 --> 00:30:19,760
Pause now for your discussion.

453
00:30:27,800 --> 00:30:31,080
As you return, notice the difference
between getting started, staying

454
00:30:31,080 --> 00:30:32,800
absorbed and switching away.

455
00:30:32,800 --> 00:30:35,800
Each may call for a different kind of
support.

456
00:30:35,800 --> 00:30:39,770
Naming what you see gives you something
specific to explore with your

457
00:30:39,770 --> 00:30:40,800
young person.

458
00:30:40,800 --> 00:30:44,800
Keep the interest itself in the picture as
a source of enjoyment.

459
00:30:47,240 --> 00:30:50,640
Let's return to interoception, the body's
inside sense that we named

460
00:30:50,640 --> 00:30:51,000
earlier.

461
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:54,200
Internal signals add another part to the
picture.

462
00:30:54,200 --> 00:30:59,290
Some experiences are hard to notice or
describe even when they strongly

463
00:30:59,290 --> 00:31:00,980
affect how a person feels.

464
00:31:03,720 --> 00:31:07,120
Interoception concerns signals from inside
the body:

465
00:31:07,120 --> 00:31:11,360
temperature, hunger, thirst or needing the
bathroom.

466
00:31:11,360 --> 00:31:15,160
Alexithymia describes difficulty
identifying and describing emotions

467
00:31:15,160 --> 00:31:16,520
and feelings.

468
00:31:16,520 --> 00:31:20,240
They're related ideas, but noticing a body
signal and knowing how to

469
00:31:20,240 --> 00:31:21,600
describe an emotion

470
00:31:21,600 --> 00:31:23,320
are different tasks.

471
00:31:23,320 --> 00:31:26,800
A young person might notice hunger only
when it becomes intense,

472
00:31:26,800 --> 00:31:30,720
or find it hard to tell whether a fluttery
feeling before a school camp

473
00:31:30,720 --> 00:31:33,600
or party is excitement or worry.

474
00:31:33,600 --> 00:31:38,400
These examples give us questions to
explore, rather than a diagnosis.

475
00:31:38,400 --> 00:31:42,080
We can offer time and ways to communicate
what is happening inside,

476
00:31:42,080 --> 00:31:44,280
without insisting on an immediate answer.

477
00:31:47,160 --> 00:31:50,760
Sometimes a build-up is happening inside
even when the surface doesn't

478
00:31:50,760 --> 00:31:51,280
show it.

479
00:31:51,280 --> 00:31:56,000
Early signs may be subtle, individual or
simply unavailable to us.

480
00:31:56,000 --> 00:32:01,080
This is an invitation to learn together,
never a reason to blame

481
00:32:01,080 --> 00:32:03,400
yourself for not noticing sooner.

482
00:32:03,400 --> 00:32:08,080
Over time, your young person and the
people who know them may build a

483
00:32:08,080 --> 00:32:09,480
shared picture of what helps.

484
00:32:09,480 --> 00:32:14,240
Describing changes clearly can help family
members and school respond

485
00:32:14,240 --> 00:32:15,440
more consistently.

486
00:32:15,960 --> 00:32:19,460
The picture should stay open to what your
young person communicates and

487
00:32:19,460 --> 00:32:21,440
to things we haven't understood yet.

488
00:32:21,440 --> 00:32:24,930
Include your young person in building that
shared picture where they

489
00:32:24,930 --> 00:32:26,160
want to be involved.

490
00:32:28,120 --> 00:32:31,880
Stay on page seven for Reading the signs.

491
00:32:31,880 --> 00:32:34,840
What changes do you notice before your
young person

492
00:32:34,840 --> 00:32:36,840
becomes overwhelmed?

493
00:32:36,840 --> 00:32:40,560
Describe what you can see or hear, as
specifically as you can.

494
00:32:40,560 --> 00:32:44,080
What would be useful for another carer or
school to know?

495
00:32:44,080 --> 00:32:47,360
You might also write what you aren't sure
about yet.

496
00:32:47,360 --> 00:32:49,640
Take a little private thinking time,

497
00:32:49,640 --> 00:32:51,800
then share only if you wish.

498
00:32:51,800 --> 00:32:54,440
Pause the video while you make those
notes.

499
00:33:02,480 --> 00:33:06,920
Keep that description available for our
work on school partnership.

500
00:33:06,920 --> 00:33:11,120
A useful note helps someone recognise a
change and respond with support.

501
00:33:11,120 --> 00:33:14,820
It doesn't need to explain everything, and
it can change as you learn

502
00:33:14,820 --> 00:33:15,440
more.

503
00:33:15,440 --> 00:33:19,920
We've now considered sensory experience,
thinking and internal signals

504
00:33:19,920 --> 00:33:20,360
as connected

505
00:33:20,360 --> 00:33:22,920
parts of the same person's day.

506
00:33:22,920 --> 00:33:26,190
Check with your young person about what
they are comfortable for others,

507
00:33:26,190 --> 00:33:28,200
including school, to know.

508
00:33:30,360 --> 00:33:33,040
That's our second session. After morning
tea, we'll continue with

509
00:33:33,040 --> 00:33:34,120
communication,

510
00:33:34,120 --> 00:33:39,000
connection and managing daily capacity.
Take a break now if you need

511
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:39,240
one.

512
00:33:39,240 --> 00:33:42,400
When you return, bring the same young
person to mind,

513
00:33:42,400 --> 00:33:45,480
along with the strengths and questions
you've been gathering.

514
00:33:52,440 --> 00:33:53,440
Welcome back.

515
00:33:53,440 --> 00:33:57,440
This session continues the picture we've
been building.

516
00:33:57,440 --> 00:34:00,770
Sensory experience, thinking and internal
signals can all shape

517
00:34:00,770 --> 00:34:01,440
communication,

518
00:34:01,440 --> 00:34:04,440
connection and the energy available across
a day.

519
00:34:04,440 --> 00:34:09,440
Keep your own young person in mind as we
explore these together.

520
00:34:11,200 --> 00:34:14,080
Let's begin with communication.

521
00:34:14,080 --> 00:34:16,560
Before thinking about anyone else's way of
communicating,

522
00:34:16,560 --> 00:34:18,080
we'll notice how many different ways

523
00:34:18,080 --> 00:34:19,640
we already use ourselves.

524
00:34:22,280 --> 00:34:25,560
Since you woke up today, how have you
communicated?

525
00:34:25,560 --> 00:34:27,480
Think of three different ways.

526
00:34:27,480 --> 00:34:30,400
It could be a spoken greeting, a message,
a wave,

527
00:34:30,400 --> 00:34:32,320
a look, pointing to something,

528
00:34:32,320 --> 00:34:34,880
or taking a moment before responding.

529
00:34:34,880 --> 00:34:36,320
Have a quiet think,

530
00:34:36,320 --> 00:34:39,160
then share one example if you'd like to.

531
00:34:39,160 --> 00:34:40,400
Pause here for a moment.

532
00:34:48,560 --> 00:34:52,160
We all move between different ways of
communicating.

533
00:34:52,160 --> 00:34:54,280
Speech is one part of that picture.

534
00:34:54,280 --> 00:35:00,070
Writing, gesture, a device, showing a
photo or moving away can carry a

535
00:35:00,070 --> 00:35:01,560
message too.

536
00:35:01,560 --> 00:35:04,490
Keep that variety in mind when we consider
what helps someone

537
00:35:04,490 --> 00:35:07,180
understand and express themselves.

538
00:35:08,720 --> 00:35:12,560
The two sides of this slide separate
understanding, information coming

539
00:35:12,560 --> 00:35:14,080
in, from expressing,

540
00:35:14,080 --> 00:35:17,440
messages going out. How much someone
speaks doesn't tell us how much

541
00:35:17,440 --> 00:35:19,160
they understand.

542
00:35:19,160 --> 00:35:23,290
A person may speak fluently and still need
support to understand spoken

543
00:35:23,290 --> 00:35:24,320
information.

544
00:35:24,320 --> 00:35:28,720
Someone else may understand more than they
can readily express.

545
00:35:28,720 --> 00:35:32,880
A A C means augmentative and alternative
communication.

546
00:35:32,880 --> 00:35:37,130
It can include signs, a communication
board or book, typing or a

547
00:35:37,130 --> 00:35:38,960
communication device.

548
00:35:38,960 --> 00:35:43,120
Some people use it alongside speech, and
some instead of speech.

549
00:35:43,120 --> 00:35:46,580
Communication can change with the setting,
the demands and how someone

550
00:35:46,580 --> 00:35:47,520
is feeling.

551
00:35:47,520 --> 00:35:51,360
Repeated words or familiar scripts can
carry meaning too.

552
00:35:51,360 --> 00:35:53,840
Stay curious about their purpose and the
context.

553
00:35:55,560 --> 00:35:58,320
As you watch this video, notice what
supports communication.

554
00:35:58,320 --> 00:36:00,640
Choose one idea that stands out:

555
00:36:00,640 --> 00:36:03,320
perhaps a communication method, something
another person does,

556
00:36:03,320 --> 00:36:05,360
or something about the environment.

557
00:36:05,360 --> 00:36:07,120
Let's hear the speakers' own accounts.

558
00:41:41,600 --> 00:41:45,200
What stood out to you about what supported
communication?

559
00:41:45,200 --> 00:41:47,360
Take a moment to think, or share one
observation

560
00:41:47,360 --> 00:41:48,680
with someone nearby.

561
00:41:48,680 --> 00:41:51,080
Stay close to what the speakers said.

562
00:41:51,080 --> 00:41:52,560
Pause here if you'd like longer.

563
00:42:00,640 --> 00:42:03,570
Keep that observation with you. There is
no single Autistic

564
00:42:03,570 --> 00:42:05,040
communication profile.

565
00:42:05,040 --> 00:42:09,800
We'll now consider the people and places
in your young person's own

566
00:42:09,800 --> 00:42:10,440
communication world.

567
00:42:12,640 --> 00:42:15,140
Turn to the communication network on page
nine.

568
00:42:15,140 --> 00:42:17,620
Your young person is in the centre.

569
00:42:17,620 --> 00:42:22,400
Around them are home, school, friends,
community and supports.

570
00:42:22,400 --> 00:42:24,680
Add one person or place now.

571
00:42:24,680 --> 00:42:27,900
You can write, draw or use a few words.

572
00:42:27,900 --> 00:42:30,280
There is no need to fill every box.

573
00:42:30,280 --> 00:42:34,580
A network can be small, changing or still
developing.

574
00:42:34,580 --> 00:42:39,920
A grandparent, teacher, coach or friend
online may see different parts

575
00:42:39,920 --> 00:42:41,020
of communication.

576
00:42:42,040 --> 00:42:45,510
In secondary school, several teachers and
a year coordinator may be

577
00:42:45,510 --> 00:42:45,960
involved.

578
00:42:45,960 --> 00:42:49,780
You may not know everyone in the network,
and that is understandable.

579
00:42:49,780 --> 00:42:52,600
Pause while you add one starting point.

580
00:43:00,960 --> 00:43:04,360
You can return to the rest later,
including with your young person and

581
00:43:04,360 --> 00:43:06,240
other communication partners.

582
00:43:06,240 --> 00:43:10,390
This page helps us notice who is connected
and what they might need to

583
00:43:10,390 --> 00:43:11,360
understand.

584
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:14,960
There is no ranking of who knows your
young person best.

585
00:43:17,120 --> 00:43:20,520
On the same page, let's think about what
useful knowledge could travel

586
00:43:20,520 --> 00:43:22,000
between people and places.

587
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:24,400
Purple is a communication strength.

588
00:43:24,400 --> 00:43:27,240
Blue is something that helps
communication.

589
00:43:27,240 --> 00:43:29,740
Orange is something you would like others
to know.

590
00:43:29,740 --> 00:43:33,740
Use coloured notes if you have them, or
the three workbook boxes.

591
00:43:33,740 --> 00:43:35,640
Write one, two or all three.

592
00:43:35,640 --> 00:43:39,700
You can use a made-up example, and you can
keep your notes private.

593
00:43:39,700 --> 00:43:43,880
If you share at a table or on a board,
leave out identifying details.

594
00:43:43,880 --> 00:43:46,516
Pause now for writing and optional
sharing.

595
00:43:54,520 --> 00:43:56,440
Look across those ideas.

596
00:43:56,440 --> 00:44:00,000
What strengths might be missed if we
looked only for speech?

597
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:03,230
Which supports involve changing what a
communication partner does or

598
00:44:03,230 --> 00:44:04,320
provides?

599
00:44:04,320 --> 00:44:10,000
Keep a blue note, or something from the
What helps box, in mind.

600
00:44:10,000 --> 00:44:12,360
That's our link to the next slide.

601
00:44:12,360 --> 00:44:15,660
Useful information can help a young person
avoid having to start again

602
00:44:15,660 --> 00:44:17,240
with every new person.

603
00:44:17,240 --> 00:44:19,400
Check with your young person what they
want

604
00:44:19,400 --> 00:44:22,840
shared about them and who they want to
know.

605
00:44:24,400 --> 00:44:27,640
Here are three options to consider
alongside what you've written.

606
00:44:27,640 --> 00:44:32,690
Clear communication means familiar,
specific words and one idea or

607
00:44:32,690 --> 00:44:34,600
step at a time.

608
00:44:34,600 --> 00:44:38,120
Processing time means allowing space to
understand,

609
00:44:38,120 --> 00:44:41,400
organise a response and communicate it.

610
00:44:41,400 --> 00:44:45,480
Visual supports mean showing information
as well as saying it.

611
00:44:45,480 --> 00:44:48,520
The useful support depends on the person
and the moment.

612
00:44:48,520 --> 00:44:52,280
Try leaving a pause without filling it
with more words.

613
00:44:52,280 --> 00:44:55,400
Keep the person's communication methods
available,

614
00:44:55,400 --> 00:44:57,920
including when the message is no.

615
00:44:57,920 --> 00:45:00,800
These are ways to make communication more
accessible,

616
00:45:00,800 --> 00:45:02,880
and we need to check whether they help.

617
00:45:04,640 --> 00:45:08,560
Visual supports are information you can
see and come back to.

618
00:45:08,560 --> 00:45:12,840
On this slide, a timer answers how much
time is left.

619
00:45:12,840 --> 00:45:16,400
A sequence answers what happens next.

620
00:45:16,400 --> 00:45:18,880
A daily schedule could answer what is
happening today,

621
00:45:18,880 --> 00:45:23,360
although that example space is blank in
the supplied slide.

622
00:45:23,360 --> 00:45:25,560
You might already use a shopping list,

623
00:45:25,560 --> 00:45:27,960
calendar or reminder on your phone.

624
00:45:27,960 --> 00:45:30,960
Your young person may use a phone, planner

625
00:45:30,960 --> 00:45:33,040
or school portal for the same purpose.

626
00:45:34,000 --> 00:45:36,200
What visual information do you use at
home,

627
00:45:36,200 --> 00:45:37,560
and what makes it helpful?

628
00:45:37,560 --> 00:45:43,240
Share a brief example if you wish, or note
one privately.

629
00:45:43,240 --> 00:45:44,160
Pause here.

630
00:45:52,560 --> 00:45:55,960
The helpful format differs from person to
person.

631
00:45:55,960 --> 00:45:59,820
It might be an object, photo, symbol or
written words, on paper or a

632
00:45:59,820 --> 00:46:01,120
device.

633
00:46:01,120 --> 00:46:05,080
Check that the information makes sense and
is available when needed.

634
00:46:05,080 --> 00:46:08,680
A visual should offer information.
Communication tools still need to

635
00:46:08,680 --> 00:46:10,320
stay available in their own right.

636
00:46:12,520 --> 00:46:16,880
Connection involves understanding in both
directions.

637
00:46:16,880 --> 00:46:20,860
The double empathy idea, introduced by
Autistic researcher

638
00:46:20,860 --> 00:46:24,120
Damian Milton, asks us to consider
misunderstandings

639
00:46:24,120 --> 00:46:26,160
between people with different experiences

640
00:46:26,160 --> 00:46:29,040
and communication styles as a shared gap.

641
00:46:29,040 --> 00:46:32,840
The image shows two people sending and
receiving

642
00:46:32,840 --> 00:46:35,920
in their own ways. Both have something to
understand.

643
00:46:35,920 --> 00:46:37,920
You may already do this at home as you
learn

644
00:46:37,920 --> 00:46:41,640
your young person's communication and they
learn yours.

645
00:46:41,640 --> 00:46:45,000
We can ask what we, as communication
partners,

646
00:46:45,000 --> 00:46:48,120
might change to make the exchange easier.

647
00:46:48,120 --> 00:46:50,600
Listening and adjustment belong on both
sides.

648
00:46:52,400 --> 00:46:56,520
Let's hear one person's experience of
being social.

649
00:46:56,520 --> 00:46:59,820
As you listen, notice what connection
means in this account, without

650
00:46:59,820 --> 00:47:01,080
expecting it to describe

651
00:47:01,080 --> 00:47:03,777
every Autistic person's experience.

652
00:47:50,840 --> 00:47:52,340
Take a quiet moment.

653
00:47:52,340 --> 00:47:53,700
What did you notice?

654
00:47:53,700 --> 00:47:56,560
You can share one reflection or hold it
privately.

655
00:47:56,560 --> 00:47:58,580
Pause the video if you need longer.

656
00:48:06,600 --> 00:48:09,800
Carry that reflection into the next
discussion.

657
00:48:09,800 --> 00:48:12,800
Connection can take different forms, and
the conditions around it

658
00:48:12,800 --> 00:48:14,600
can make a difference.

659
00:48:16,160 --> 00:48:20,560
Connection can happen in family life,
school, the community and online.

660
00:48:20,560 --> 00:48:25,720
It might be one-to-one conversation,
gaming alongside someone, a group

661
00:48:25,720 --> 00:48:26,160
chat,

662
00:48:26,160 --> 00:48:29,160
sharing a lot about an interest or simply
being together.

663
00:48:29,160 --> 00:48:34,160
Some relationships involve irregular
contact and still matter.

664
00:48:34,160 --> 00:48:38,160
Online friendships can be real and
important connections.

665
00:48:38,160 --> 00:48:41,160
Families and cultures also have different
ways of being together.

666
00:48:41,160 --> 00:48:45,630
Ask what connection feels meaningful to
your young person and what

667
00:48:45,630 --> 00:48:46,160
conditions help.

668
00:48:46,160 --> 00:48:50,160
We can respect their preferences while
continuing the conversations

669
00:48:50,160 --> 00:48:53,160
about safety and support that matter in
any setting.

670
00:48:55,520 --> 00:48:59,320
On page ten, think about when your young
person seems most comfortable

671
00:48:59,320 --> 00:48:59,860
connecting.

672
00:48:59,860 --> 00:49:02,320
What do they enjoy:

673
00:49:02,320 --> 00:49:07,640
talking, gaming, sport, making something,
shared interests, or being

674
00:49:07,640 --> 00:49:08,920
together quietly?

675
00:49:08,920 --> 00:49:13,190
When does it feel enjoyable, and when does
it become tiring or

676
00:49:13,190 --> 00:49:14,540
overwhelming?

677
00:49:14,540 --> 00:49:18,670
Then consider what adults could change to
make connections safer,

678
00:49:18,670 --> 00:49:20,700
easier or more meaningful.

679
00:49:20,700 --> 00:49:23,920
Start with your young person's
preferences.

680
00:49:24,920 --> 00:49:27,220
You can reflect privately or talk with
your table.

681
00:49:27,220 --> 00:49:28,980
Pause for the discussion.

682
00:49:37,160 --> 00:49:40,760
As we return, keep one condition that
seems to help in mind.

683
00:49:40,760 --> 00:49:44,920
It could be the person, place, activity or
amount of time.

684
00:49:44,920 --> 00:49:47,120
Some young people have few connections
right now,

685
00:49:47,120 --> 00:49:49,920
and this is not a comparison between
families.

686
00:49:49,920 --> 00:49:51,600
Meaningful connection can look different

687
00:49:51,600 --> 00:49:53,640
across people, settings and days.

688
00:49:55,160 --> 00:49:56,960
Now let's look at daily capacity.

689
00:49:56,960 --> 00:49:59,700
Something can be enjoyable and still take
energy.

690
00:49:59,700 --> 00:50:02,000
Communication, sensory processing,

691
00:50:02,000 --> 00:50:04,000
transitions and social connection

692
00:50:04,000 --> 00:50:06,900
all draw on the resources available in
that moment.

693
00:50:08,720 --> 00:50:11,880
Think of energy as a battery. Each day can
begin with a different

694
00:50:11,880 --> 00:50:14,420
amount of charge, and activities draw on
it.

695
00:50:14,420 --> 00:50:17,820
When it runs low, ordinary things can
become harder.

696
00:50:17,820 --> 00:50:22,080
Many people also use the spoons analogy: a
limited supply of energy,

697
00:50:22,080 --> 00:50:25,080
with different activities using different
amounts.

698
00:50:25,080 --> 00:50:29,920
These are optional ways to describe an
experience, rather than measures

699
00:50:29,920 --> 00:50:30,960
of ability.

700
00:50:30,960 --> 00:50:35,940
The important question is what costs
energy and what helps restore it.

701
00:50:36,780 --> 00:50:40,370
Rest, familiar interests or a
better-fitting environment may help

702
00:50:40,370 --> 00:50:41,140
recharge.

703
00:50:41,140 --> 00:50:45,470
Support needs to match the charge that is
actually available, not the

704
00:50:45,470 --> 00:50:47,640
amount we hoped would be there.

705
00:50:49,520 --> 00:50:52,720
Capacity can vary across a day and from
one day to another.

706
00:50:52,720 --> 00:50:57,600
Stress, sensory demands, sleep, social
situations and other events

707
00:50:57,600 --> 00:51:00,200
can change what feels manageable.

708
00:51:00,200 --> 00:51:04,760
The same request may be easy on one
occasion and too much on another.

709
00:51:04,760 --> 00:51:07,840
Think about what seems to use your young
person's energy

710
00:51:07,840 --> 00:51:10,640
and what lightens the load.

711
00:51:10,640 --> 00:51:15,200
We don't need to interpret a change in
capacity as unwillingness.

712
00:51:15,200 --> 00:51:19,310
It gives us a reason to look again at the
demands and the support

713
00:51:19,310 --> 00:51:20,080
available.

714
00:51:20,080 --> 00:51:23,560
In secondary school, a timetable with
several teachers, assessment due

715
00:51:23,560 --> 00:51:24,040
dates

716
00:51:24,040 --> 00:51:26,880
and friendship demands can all add to that
load.

717
00:51:28,880 --> 00:51:32,220
Masking can place another demand on
energy.

718
00:51:32,220 --> 00:51:35,240
We'll hear a first-person account now.

719
00:51:35,240 --> 00:51:37,420
Let the speaker describe their own
experience,

720
00:51:37,420 --> 00:51:41,140
and leave a little room afterwards to
reflect.

721
00:53:13,600 --> 00:53:15,600
Take a moment before we move on.

722
00:53:18,720 --> 00:53:22,060
The image places two moments beside one
another:

723
00:53:22,060 --> 00:53:26,560
managing at school, and arriving home with
very little energy left.

724
00:53:26,560 --> 00:53:30,060
Appearing fine may not show the effort
underneath.

725
00:53:30,060 --> 00:53:34,100
That doesn't tell us exactly what is
happening for every young person.

726
00:53:34,100 --> 00:53:36,900
It reminds us to consider what a day
costs,

727
00:53:36,900 --> 00:53:39,700
including effort we may not see.

728
00:53:39,700 --> 00:53:43,850
Changes to the environment and
expectations can sometimes reduce that

729
00:53:43,850 --> 00:53:44,920
effort.

730
00:53:44,920 --> 00:53:48,580
We'll return to the after-school
experience in our next session.

731
00:53:50,120 --> 00:53:52,600
The teenage years can add pressure to fit
in.

732
00:53:52,600 --> 00:53:57,280
A young person may spend more energy
hiding parts of themselves while

733
00:53:57,280 --> 00:53:59,300
also trying to work out who they are.

734
00:53:59,300 --> 00:54:04,080
The effort behind appearing fine can grow
across school and social life.

735
00:54:04,080 --> 00:54:08,800
For some young people, sustained overload
contributes to burnout or

736
00:54:08,800 --> 00:54:10,900
makes school inaccessible.

737
00:54:10,900 --> 00:54:13,560
We'll explore those ideas next session.

738
00:54:13,560 --> 00:54:16,120
There is also room for growth:

739
00:54:16,760 --> 00:54:20,850
when home and school feel safer, young
people can develop self-understanding

740
00:54:20,850 --> 00:54:22,620
and speak up about what helps.

741
00:54:22,620 --> 00:54:27,100
Stay open to their account, and take
concerns about wellbeing seriously.

742
00:54:27,100 --> 00:54:29,780
You do not have to work those concerns out
alone.

743
00:54:32,360 --> 00:54:36,150
On page ten, consider where your young
person seems to work hard to

744
00:54:36,150 --> 00:54:36,900
appear fine,

745
00:54:36,900 --> 00:54:40,060
and where they seem most comfortable being
themselves.

746
00:54:40,060 --> 00:54:44,900
What could adults change to help them feel
more supported?

747
00:54:44,900 --> 00:54:47,700
Start with private thinking.

748
00:54:47,700 --> 00:54:52,080
You do not need to prove masking is
occurring, or share a personal story.

749
00:54:52,080 --> 00:54:55,480
Keep your notes descriptive and your
questions open.

750
00:54:55,480 --> 00:54:59,680
If you wish, discuss one practical change
with your table.

751
00:54:59,680 --> 00:55:00,980
Pause here.

752
00:55:09,960 --> 00:55:13,120
Keep that possible change in mind.

753
00:55:13,120 --> 00:55:16,840
Recognising something now doesn't mean you
should have known it earlier.

754
00:55:16,840 --> 00:55:20,040
We can learn from new understanding
without blame.

755
00:55:20,040 --> 00:55:24,040
Making room for a person to be themselves
connects directly to the idea

756
00:55:24,040 --> 00:55:25,400
on our closing slide.

757
00:55:28,440 --> 00:55:31,430
Across these two sessions, we've been
building a picture of your young

758
00:55:31,430 --> 00:55:32,440
person.

759
00:55:32,440 --> 00:55:36,810
How the world arrives through their
senses. How they notice detail,

760
00:55:36,810 --> 00:55:38,040
focus deeply

761
00:55:38,040 --> 00:55:42,200
and bring particular strengths. How they
experience internal signals,

762
00:55:42,200 --> 00:55:43,080
communicate

763
00:55:43,080 --> 00:55:47,880
and reach towards other people. Managing
those demands every day can

764
00:55:47,880 --> 00:55:48,360
take a great

765
00:55:48,360 --> 00:55:52,820
deal of energy, especially when the
surroundings don't fit. Sensory

766
00:55:52,820 --> 00:55:53,560
load,

767
00:55:53,560 --> 00:55:57,080
transitions, communication across
different styles, and sometimes

768
00:55:57,080 --> 00:55:58,200
appearing fine can

769
00:55:58,200 --> 00:56:02,440
all add to the cost. A young person who
knows who they are, and feels

770
00:56:02,440 --> 00:56:03,720
valued for it,

771
00:56:03,720 --> 00:56:08,360
has more room for learning, connection and
self-understanding. Belonging

772
00:56:08,360 --> 00:56:08,840
matters.

773
00:56:08,840 --> 00:56:13,110
So does genuine choice about how they
communicate, where they feel

774
00:56:13,110 --> 00:56:13,640
comfortable

775
00:56:13,640 --> 00:56:17,800
and when they need a break. Knowing other
Autistic people can offer

776
00:56:17,800 --> 00:56:18,120
connection and

777
00:56:18,120 --> 00:56:22,280
a sense of shared experience. Think back
to the strengths and joys you

778
00:56:22,280 --> 00:56:23,160
wrote down this morning.

779
00:56:23,160 --> 00:56:27,410
Supporting a positive Autistic identity
can begin with listening,

780
00:56:27,410 --> 00:56:28,040
making room for

781
00:56:28,040 --> 00:56:31,720
those strengths, and showing that this
whole person is worth knowing.

782
00:56:31,720 --> 00:56:35,520
We'll pause here. Take your lunch break
when you're ready. When we

783
00:56:35,520 --> 00:56:36,360
return, we'll consider

784
00:56:36,360 --> 00:56:40,520
what this understanding can look like at
home and in the community.

785
00:56:50,720 --> 00:56:52,220
Welcome back.

786
00:56:52,220 --> 00:56:55,360
This morning we built our understanding of
Autistic experience.

787
00:56:55,360 --> 00:56:59,460
Now we consider everyday life with a
teenager: distress and recovery,

788
00:56:59,460 --> 00:57:03,460
homework, interests, growing independence,
sleep and screens.

789
00:57:03,460 --> 00:57:07,140
You know your family, and your young
person has their own knowledge

790
00:57:07,140 --> 00:57:11,000
and preferences too. Take what fits and
leave what doesn't.

791
00:57:11,000 --> 00:57:14,580
We are looking for workable supports and
small steps

792
00:57:14,580 --> 00:57:16,912
you can consider together.

793
00:57:18,440 --> 00:57:21,480
What we can see and hear may be only the
tip of the iceberg.

794
00:57:21,480 --> 00:57:25,200
Underneath can sit thoughts, feelings,
values and needs, including

795
00:57:25,200 --> 00:57:25,640
belonging,

796
00:57:25,640 --> 00:57:28,520
predictability, security and
self-expression.

797
00:57:28,520 --> 00:57:31,160
Think privately of a hard moment.

798
00:57:31,160 --> 00:57:33,560
What had happened beforehand?

799
00:57:33,560 --> 00:57:38,060
Perhaps there was an unexpected change, a
long day of different

800
00:57:38,060 --> 00:57:38,560
teachers and

801
00:57:38,560 --> 00:57:42,120
rooms, or a request that was simply too
big for that day.

802
00:57:42,120 --> 00:57:44,960
We may not know the answer straight away.

803
00:57:45,760 --> 00:57:49,440
Behaviour can give us information, and
curiosity helps us look beyond the

804
00:57:49,440 --> 00:57:50,000
surface.

805
00:57:50,000 --> 00:57:53,640
This isn't about blaming a young person or
a parent. It is about

806
00:57:53,640 --> 00:57:53,920
understanding

807
00:57:53,920 --> 00:57:56,480
the conditions around a difficult moment.

808
00:57:56,480 --> 00:58:01,640
With teenagers, distress may show up as
withdrawal, anger or a door

809
00:58:01,640 --> 00:58:02,480
being slammed.

810
00:58:02,480 --> 00:58:05,080
Keep the same curiosity about the load
underneath.

811
00:58:06,960 --> 00:58:09,920
Overwhelm can turn inward or outward.

812
00:58:09,920 --> 00:58:13,240
A shutdown may look like becoming very
still, freezing,

813
00:58:13,240 --> 00:58:15,720
withdrawing to a room or being less able
to speak

814
00:58:15,720 --> 00:58:17,880
and explain what is needed.

815
00:58:17,880 --> 00:58:21,360
A meltdown may involve crying, running
away

816
00:58:21,360 --> 00:58:24,480
or a large outward expression of distress.

817
00:58:24,480 --> 00:58:26,960
These are involuntary responses to
overwhelm.

818
00:58:26,960 --> 00:58:30,320
Quietness can be easy to miss,

819
00:58:30,320 --> 00:58:32,880
especially when someone seems to be
managing.

820
00:58:32,880 --> 00:58:36,120
The response alone may not tell us the
full story.

821
00:58:36,120 --> 00:58:38,160
Keep the person's safety and need

822
00:58:38,160 --> 00:58:39,880
for reduced demands in view,

823
00:58:39,880 --> 00:58:43,080
and allow some space around these
experiences.

824
00:58:43,080 --> 00:58:45,280
They can be hard for families to revisit.

825
00:58:47,680 --> 00:58:50,360
Sometimes overload builds over a long
period.

826
00:58:50,360 --> 00:58:53,440
Autistic burnout describes deep physical,
mental

827
00:58:53,440 --> 00:58:55,880
and emotional exhaustion, with a reduction

828
00:58:55,880 --> 00:58:58,240
in capacity that may last.

829
00:58:58,240 --> 00:59:00,360
School Can't describes being unable to
attend

830
00:59:00,360 --> 00:59:02,160
because school has become overwhelming

831
00:59:02,160 --> 00:59:06,080
through anxiety, fear or sensory load.

832
00:59:06,080 --> 00:59:08,720
These terms help us consider what someone
may have been

833
00:59:08,720 --> 00:59:12,200
carrying and what support or recovery they
need.

834
00:59:12,200 --> 00:59:14,600
They don't let us diagnose an individual
situation

835
00:59:14,600 --> 00:59:15,920
from a slide.

836
00:59:15,920 --> 00:59:19,560
If your family is living through this, it
can be very hard.

837
00:59:19,560 --> 00:59:21,960
A conversation with appropriate health and
school supports

838
00:59:21,960 --> 00:59:24,880
can help explore the individual picture.

839
00:59:24,880 --> 00:59:26,960
We'll look at working with school in
Session Five.

840
00:59:28,480 --> 00:59:34,400
In the moment, try reducing input: fewer
words, fewer questions and

841
00:59:34,400 --> 00:59:35,040
less noise.

842
00:59:35,040 --> 00:59:39,960
A quiet, safe space works best when you've
agreed it with your young

843
00:59:39,960 --> 00:59:41,520
person beforehand, while everyone

844
00:59:41,520 --> 00:59:46,670
is calm. Keep your approach matched to
what you know helps your young

845
00:59:46,670 --> 00:59:49,360
person. A distressed person

846
00:59:49,360 --> 00:59:53,240
may have very little capacity for
explanation or problem-solving. The

847
00:59:53,240 --> 00:59:54,640
conversation about what

848
00:59:54,640 --> 00:59:59,040
happened can wait until they are ready. On
page twelve, there's room to

849
00:59:59,040 --> 01:00:00,320
gather early signs,

850
01:00:00,320 --> 01:00:05,080
what helps in the moment and what helps
recovery. You can add to that

851
01:00:05,080 --> 01:00:06,320
picture over time.

852
01:00:06,320 --> 01:00:11,540
These are options to consider, rather than
a script every family has to

853
01:00:11,540 --> 01:00:12,400
follow.

854
01:00:14,080 --> 01:00:17,420
Co-regulation means a trusted adult
helping someone settle through a

855
01:00:17,420 --> 01:00:19,280
steady, responsive presence.

856
01:00:19,280 --> 01:00:23,510
Teenagers can still need that support,
although it may look different

857
01:00:23,510 --> 01:00:23,880
now.

858
01:00:23,880 --> 01:00:28,010
It might be sitting nearby, a quiet drive
or being available without

859
01:00:28,010 --> 01:00:30,080
many words. Respect their need for space.

860
01:00:30,080 --> 01:00:35,870
Self-regulation involves the ways a person
manages their own energy and

861
01:00:35,870 --> 01:00:36,580
feelings,

862
01:00:36,580 --> 01:00:38,980
with support that develops over time.

863
01:00:38,980 --> 01:00:42,780
Nobody stays calm every time, especially
when exhausted.

864
01:00:43,080 --> 01:00:44,780
Your own capacity matters too.

865
01:00:44,780 --> 01:00:48,710
We can look for support without expecting
either you or your young

866
01:00:48,710 --> 01:00:50,380
person to manage perfectly.

867
01:00:52,480 --> 01:00:56,240
An after-school crash can continue into
the teenage years and sometimes

868
01:00:56,240 --> 01:00:58,880
become more noticeable as the demands
grow.

869
01:00:58,880 --> 01:01:02,990
A young person may appear to manage at
school, then arrive home

870
01:01:02,990 --> 01:01:05,800
exhausted or go straight to their room.

871
01:01:05,800 --> 01:01:09,420
Both accounts can be real parts of the
same day.

872
01:01:09,420 --> 01:01:14,650
Recovery might involve food, quiet,
movement, a screen or time with an

873
01:01:14,650 --> 01:01:15,620
interest.

874
01:01:15,620 --> 01:01:21,040
Let questions and homework wait until
there is more capacity.

875
01:01:22,060 --> 01:01:25,820
A predictable routine or a quiet car ride
may help.

876
01:01:25,820 --> 01:01:29,330
Connection may happen later, through a
shared game, a drive or a

877
01:01:29,330 --> 01:01:31,240
conversation in the evening.

878
01:01:31,240 --> 01:01:34,920
Give space without disappearing from their
support network.

879
01:01:34,920 --> 01:01:40,250
If withdrawal does not ease or you are
concerned about wellbeing, seek

880
01:01:40,250 --> 01:01:43,040
support to understand what is happening
for them.

881
01:01:44,840 --> 01:01:48,510
Homework brings subject work and the
demands of starting, planning and

882
01:01:48,510 --> 01:01:49,680
keeping track.

883
01:01:49,680 --> 01:01:54,250
Agree together when it will happen,
allowing for recovery, and where it

884
01:01:54,250 --> 01:01:55,240
works best.

885
01:01:55,240 --> 01:01:59,840
Sometimes a person sitting nearby while
doing their own task makes the

886
01:01:59,840 --> 01:02:01,240
start easier.

887
01:02:01,240 --> 01:02:05,020
Make due dates and the steps between them
visible in a format your

888
01:02:05,020 --> 01:02:06,640
young person can use.

889
01:02:06,640 --> 01:02:10,440
Break a larger task into smaller parts
with a clear finish.

890
01:02:10,440 --> 01:02:14,240
This can support the planning while the
work stays theirs.

891
01:02:14,240 --> 01:02:18,440
If the load repeatedly exceeds their
capacity, bring that information

892
01:02:18,440 --> 01:02:20,840
to school and ask about adjustments.

893
01:02:20,840 --> 01:02:24,040
Families often do a great deal of
organising already.

894
01:02:24,040 --> 01:02:28,030
Look for a small support that makes the
task more manageable for both

895
01:02:28,030 --> 01:02:28,640
of you.

896
01:02:30,400 --> 01:02:34,600
Interests can be an anchor for regulation,
connection and joy.

897
01:02:34,600 --> 01:02:37,720
Time with a familiar interest may help
restore energy.

898
01:02:37,720 --> 01:02:40,640
It can also offer a way into your young
person's world:

899
01:02:40,640 --> 01:02:42,600
listening while they explain,

900
01:02:42,600 --> 01:02:45,800
watching what they make or joining in when
invited.

901
01:02:45,800 --> 01:02:49,040
An interest is part of their experience
and identity.

902
01:02:49,040 --> 01:02:51,280
Taking it away as a consequence can also

903
01:02:51,280 --> 01:02:53,280
take away a source of regulation.

904
01:02:53,280 --> 01:02:55,040
If that approach has been familiar at
home,

905
01:02:55,040 --> 01:02:57,880
you can consider the function the interest
serves

906
01:02:57,880 --> 01:03:00,680
and what another response might look like.

907
01:03:00,680 --> 01:03:02,840
Stay curious about what it gives your
young person.

908
01:03:02,840 --> 01:03:05,960
For many teenagers, an interest is also
where their friends

909
01:03:05,960 --> 01:03:07,960
and online communities are.

910
01:03:07,960 --> 01:03:09,640
You don't have to be good at their game

911
01:03:09,640 --> 01:03:11,800
to show interest in what it means to them.

912
01:03:13,400 --> 01:03:17,240
Independence can grow in small, supported
steps.

913
01:03:17,240 --> 01:03:21,400
There is no birthday by which everyone has
to do the same things.

914
01:03:21,400 --> 01:03:25,280
The useful step might involve an alarm, a
little money,

915
01:03:25,280 --> 01:03:29,840
part of a journey or communicating a
preference at an appointment.

916
01:03:29,840 --> 01:03:31,560
Talk about risk together.

917
01:03:31,560 --> 01:03:33,320
What feels safe enough?

918
01:03:33,320 --> 01:03:35,560
What support stays in place,

919
01:03:35,560 --> 01:03:38,240
and what happens if the plan changes?

920
01:03:38,240 --> 01:03:40,960
Support can change as confidence grows,

921
01:03:40,960 --> 01:03:43,320
and it can remain where it is needed.

922
01:03:43,320 --> 01:03:45,760
Home can continue to be a safe base

923
01:03:45,760 --> 01:03:48,200
where your young person can be themselves

924
01:03:48,200 --> 01:03:50,400
while exploring more of the world.

925
01:03:52,440 --> 01:03:55,680
Sleep and screens can raise difficult
questions for families.

926
01:03:55,680 --> 01:04:00,470
Tiredness affects capacity, and some
Autistic young people find sleep

927
01:04:00,470 --> 01:04:01,080
hard.

928
01:04:01,080 --> 01:04:05,780
At the same time, screens can offer rest,
regulation, interests and

929
01:04:05,780 --> 01:04:06,680
real connection.

930
01:04:06,680 --> 01:04:10,220
Look at what the screen is doing for your
young person as well as what

931
01:04:10,220 --> 01:04:11,160
worries you.

932
01:04:11,160 --> 01:04:13,760
What matters most for your family right
now?

933
01:04:13,760 --> 01:04:18,930
Work towards an agreement together, and
choose a time to review how it

934
01:04:18,930 --> 01:04:19,760
is going.

935
01:04:20,240 --> 01:04:22,940
A general rule doesn't tell us the whole
picture.

936
01:04:22,940 --> 01:04:27,210
Keep concerns about sleep, wellbeing or
online safety open for

937
01:04:27,210 --> 01:04:28,680
appropriate support,

938
01:04:28,680 --> 01:04:31,660
while making room for your young person's
perspective.

939
01:04:34,160 --> 01:04:38,120
Turn to One step towards independence on
page twelve.

940
01:04:38,120 --> 01:04:40,280
Think of one thing your young person might
want to do

941
01:04:40,280 --> 01:04:41,800
with a little more independence.

942
01:04:41,800 --> 01:04:45,360
It might be catching a bus, preparing
food,

943
01:04:45,360 --> 01:04:48,360
managing some money or contacting a
teacher.

944
01:04:48,360 --> 01:04:51,120
What is the first small step?

945
01:04:51,120 --> 01:04:53,560
What support stays in place?

946
01:04:53,560 --> 01:04:55,040
How will you review it together?

947
01:04:55,040 --> 01:04:58,560
Make an initial note, then share one idea

948
01:04:58,560 --> 01:05:00,040
with your table if you wish.

949
01:05:00,040 --> 01:05:02,760
There is no timetable to meet,

950
01:05:02,760 --> 01:05:05,760
and a very small step can be meaningful.

951
01:05:05,760 --> 01:05:07,760
Pause now for the activity.

952
01:05:15,840 --> 01:05:18,800
Take that idea back into a conversation
with your young person.

953
01:05:18,800 --> 01:05:22,760
Check whether it is something they want,
and whether the step feels the

954
01:05:22,760 --> 01:05:23,820
right size.

955
01:05:23,820 --> 01:05:26,980
Keep the support and review in the plan.

956
01:05:26,980 --> 01:05:30,860
This could become one of your small
actions at the end of the workshop.

957
01:05:32,640 --> 01:05:36,480
Your own battery counts too. Helping
another person settle is harder

958
01:05:36,480 --> 01:05:37,040
when you are running

959
01:05:37,040 --> 01:05:42,910
on empty. On page thirteen, take a moment
with My own battery. What small

960
01:05:42,910 --> 01:05:45,040
thing helps you recharge?

961
01:05:45,040 --> 01:05:48,370
Who, if anyone, could help make this week
a little lighter? It might be a

962
01:05:48,370 --> 01:05:48,880
short walk, a

963
01:05:48,880 --> 01:05:52,830
few quiet minutes, a conversation or
practical help with a task. If

964
01:05:52,830 --> 01:05:54,000
support is scarce, that

965
01:05:54,000 --> 01:05:58,730
is a real constraint, not something you
have done wrong. Keep the idea

966
01:05:58,730 --> 01:05:59,480
realistic. Pause

967
01:05:59,480 --> 01:06:00,920
here to think or write.

968
01:06:09,400 --> 01:06:10,800
Keep whatever feels possible.

969
01:06:10,800 --> 01:06:14,580
Looking after your capacity is part of
family planning.

970
01:06:14,580 --> 01:06:19,470
Sometimes the useful step is reducing one
demand or asking for specific

971
01:06:19,470 --> 01:06:20,040
help.

972
01:06:20,040 --> 01:06:24,330
It says nothing about how much you love
your young person, and it doesn't

973
01:06:24,330 --> 01:06:27,180
have to become another task to succeed at.

974
01:06:29,400 --> 01:06:33,000
Families know a lot. Let's make room for
that knowledge.

975
01:06:33,000 --> 01:06:36,600
On page thirteen, choose one thing that
works in your home.

976
01:06:36,600 --> 01:06:40,120
It could relate to mornings, after school,
homework, sleep, screens or

977
01:06:40,120 --> 01:06:41,200
your own energy.

978
01:06:41,200 --> 01:06:45,800
At your table, each person can offer one
idea, with the option to pass.

979
01:06:45,800 --> 01:06:49,000
Listen for something that might fit your
family and write it down.

980
01:06:49,000 --> 01:06:52,000
We don't need to solve one family's whole
situation.

981
01:06:52,000 --> 01:06:55,010
If you're watching alone, note what
already works and one idea you

982
01:06:55,010 --> 01:06:56,000
might explore.

983
01:06:56,000 --> 01:06:57,600
Pause now for the sharing.

984
01:07:05,720 --> 01:07:09,320
Choose one idea to keep, and leave room to
adapt it.

985
01:07:09,320 --> 01:07:13,120
What works in another home may need to
look different in yours.

986
01:07:13,120 --> 01:07:15,240
As we move into partnership with school,

987
01:07:15,240 --> 01:07:17,920
keep one observation from home in mind.

988
01:07:17,920 --> 01:07:19,720
It may be something school needs to
understand

989
01:07:19,720 --> 01:07:21,640
about your young person's day.

990
01:07:28,560 --> 01:07:31,870
Now we turn to partnership with school.
Keep that home observation in

991
01:07:31,870 --> 01:07:32,400
mind.

992
01:07:32,400 --> 01:07:36,040
Your young person, your family and the
school may each hold different

993
01:07:36,040 --> 01:07:37,340
parts of the picture.

994
01:07:37,340 --> 01:07:42,230
Bringing those together can help shape
useful support, with your young

995
01:07:42,230 --> 01:07:44,320
person as a partner in the conversation.

996
01:07:44,320 --> 01:07:48,220
People arrive with very different school
experiences.

997
01:07:48,220 --> 01:07:51,060
Some relationships feel helpful, and
others have been difficult.

998
01:07:51,060 --> 01:07:54,780
You can take part without sharing a
personal account.

999
01:07:55,260 --> 01:07:59,110
Please keep staff, schools and young
people unidentifiable in group

1000
01:07:59,110 --> 01:07:59,700
discussion.

1001
01:08:01,760 --> 01:08:05,230
Look at the overlapping partnerships:
family, school staff and support

1002
01:08:05,230 --> 01:08:06,080
staff, with your young

1003
01:08:06,080 --> 01:08:08,080
person at the centre.

1004
01:08:08,080 --> 01:08:11,320
Each person brings different knowledge and
a different relationship.

1005
01:08:11,320 --> 01:08:13,480
Who else belongs in that picture for your
family:

1006
01:08:13,480 --> 01:08:17,240
perhaps a grandparent, therapist, tutor or
employer?

1007
01:08:17,240 --> 01:08:20,200
What is the shared purpose of these
partnerships?

1008
01:08:20,200 --> 01:08:24,160
How can your young person's own
communication help shape it?

1009
01:08:24,160 --> 01:08:27,720
Take a moment to reflect or discuss, and
pause if you'd like more time.

1010
01:08:36,360 --> 01:08:39,720
Keep your young person at the centre as
you consider the answer.

1011
01:08:39,720 --> 01:08:42,720
No single person holds the whole picture.

1012
01:08:42,720 --> 01:08:46,190
A useful partnership helps people
understand each other's knowledge and

1013
01:08:46,190 --> 01:08:46,760
agree on support

1014
01:08:46,760 --> 01:08:48,440
that makes a difference to the young
person.

1015
01:08:50,600 --> 01:08:53,960
The Family-School Partnerships Framework
recognises parents and

1016
01:08:53,960 --> 01:08:55,520
families as young people's first

1017
01:08:55,520 --> 01:08:58,080
and continuing educators.

1018
01:08:58,080 --> 01:09:02,030
Learning happens across settings, and a
family's diversity and strengths

1019
01:09:02,030 --> 01:09:03,840
matter to that learning.

1020
01:09:03,840 --> 01:09:07,520
The other principles include community
involvement, mutual trust and

1021
01:09:07,520 --> 01:09:09,240
shared responsibility, supported

1022
01:09:09,240 --> 01:09:11,840
by committed and collaborative leadership.

1023
01:09:11,840 --> 01:09:15,780
In an everyday conversation, look for
respectful listening, clear

1024
01:09:15,780 --> 01:09:17,760
information, your young person's

1025
01:09:17,760 --> 01:09:22,080
views and agreed next steps.

1026
01:09:22,080 --> 01:09:25,160
Family knowledge and school knowledge are
both needed from the

1027
01:09:25,160 --> 01:09:25,960
beginning.

1028
01:09:25,960 --> 01:09:28,450
Respectful listening is possible even when
people see a situation

1029
01:09:28,450 --> 01:09:29,000
differently.

1030
01:09:31,240 --> 01:09:33,560
What makes collaboration work?

1031
01:09:33,560 --> 01:09:36,600
Think of a situation where people have
worked well together,

1032
01:09:36,600 --> 01:09:39,600
inside or outside school.

1033
01:09:39,600 --> 01:09:42,800
What helped them listen and make a
decision?

1034
01:09:42,800 --> 01:09:46,080
Share a brief idea or reflect privately.

1035
01:09:46,080 --> 01:09:48,000
Pause here if you would like time.

1036
01:09:56,120 --> 01:10:00,290
The slide brings together shared goals and
values, mutual trust,

1037
01:10:00,290 --> 01:10:02,000
consultation, curiosity

1038
01:10:02,000 --> 01:10:04,200
and attention to different perspectives.

1039
01:10:04,200 --> 01:10:07,120
Your young person's voice belongs
throughout.

1040
01:10:07,120 --> 01:10:10,770
The phrase assume good intent may feel
difficult if trust has been

1041
01:10:10,770 --> 01:10:11,520
damaged.

1042
01:10:11,520 --> 01:10:15,400
Trust needs action from both sides, and it
can take time to rebuild.

1043
01:10:15,400 --> 01:10:19,150
A possible opening is: how could we work
together so school goes well

1044
01:10:19,150 --> 01:10:19,800
for my young

1045
01:10:19,800 --> 01:10:20,800
person?

1046
01:10:20,800 --> 01:10:25,040
That gives the conversation a purpose
while leaving room to discuss

1047
01:10:25,040 --> 01:10:26,400
what needs to change.

1048
01:10:26,400 --> 01:10:29,840
With several teachers involved, it can
help to be clear about who will

1049
01:10:29,840 --> 01:10:30,680
carry the agreed

1050
01:10:30,680 --> 01:10:32,160
information between people.

1051
01:10:34,280 --> 01:10:38,280
Power can sit in the way a meeting is
arranged, the language used and

1052
01:10:38,280 --> 01:10:39,240
who can make decisions.

1053
01:10:39,240 --> 01:10:44,530
Familiarity with school systems, cultural
and historical experiences of

1054
01:10:44,530 --> 01:10:45,160
education,

1055
01:10:45,160 --> 01:10:50,500
language and financial circumstances can
also shape how a conversation

1056
01:10:50,500 --> 01:10:51,000
feels.

1057
01:10:51,000 --> 01:10:57,590
Who sets the agenda? Whose knowledge gets
heard? Is your young person

1058
01:10:57,590 --> 01:10:58,920
included in a way that works

1059
01:10:58,920 --> 01:11:04,930
for them? What might need to change about
the process? Naming these

1060
01:11:04,930 --> 01:11:06,280
features can help us plan

1061
01:11:06,280 --> 01:11:10,020
a more balanced conversation. We can
examine the meeting itself without

1062
01:11:10,020 --> 01:11:11,560
turning the discussion

1063
01:11:11,560 --> 01:11:16,040
into a judgement about an identifiable
person.

1064
01:11:17,720 --> 01:11:22,760
Use the Power dynamics activity on page
fifteen, or three sticky notes.

1065
01:11:22,760 --> 01:11:26,240
First, describe an imbalance from a home
perspective.

1066
01:11:26,240 --> 01:11:29,160
On the second, consider one from a school
perspective.

1067
01:11:29,160 --> 01:11:33,560
On the third, suggest how the imbalance
could be addressed.

1068
01:11:33,560 --> 01:11:36,800
Thinking about another perspective doesn't
mean excusing poor practice.

1069
01:11:36,800 --> 01:11:40,760
It helps us identify what could change in
a conversation.

1070
01:11:40,760 --> 01:11:45,080
You can share at your table, add anonymous
notes under Home, School

1071
01:11:45,080 --> 01:11:48,360
and Solutions, or keep your notes private.

1072
01:11:48,360 --> 01:11:49,640
Pause now for the activity.

1073
01:11:57,680 --> 01:12:01,000
Look again at the solutions. Could the
agenda be shared earlier?

1074
01:12:01,000 --> 01:12:02,840
Could language be clearer,

1075
01:12:02,840 --> 01:12:04,960
a support person be included,

1076
01:12:04,960 --> 01:12:06,520
or another way of contributing

1077
01:12:06,520 --> 01:12:08,040
be available?

1078
01:12:08,040 --> 01:12:11,720
Choose one idea that would make
participation more workable.

1079
01:12:11,720 --> 01:12:13,960
Keep it for the conversation plan later in
this session.

1080
01:12:15,520 --> 01:12:16,880
We're going to hear from Adon.

1081
01:12:16,880 --> 01:12:21,460
As you watch, pay attention to what
decisions were made and who made

1082
01:12:21,460 --> 01:12:21,800
them.

1083
01:12:21,800 --> 01:12:26,860
Notice the effect of being included, or
not included, in decisions about

1084
01:12:26,860 --> 01:12:28,240
your own experience.

1085
01:14:23,320 --> 01:14:25,640
What impact did those decisions have on
Adon?

1086
01:14:25,640 --> 01:14:29,960
Make a note on page fifteen, or discuss
the question briefly.

1087
01:14:29,960 --> 01:14:35,300
Then consider how your young person's
views are asked for and heard at

1088
01:14:35,300 --> 01:14:35,720
school.

1089
01:14:35,720 --> 01:14:38,420
Pause here if you'd like more time.

1090
01:14:47,360 --> 01:14:50,840
Your young person's views can be spoken,
written, typed, drawn or

1091
01:14:50,840 --> 01:14:52,960
shared through a trusted person.

1092
01:14:52,960 --> 01:14:54,720
Ask what helps,

1093
01:14:54,720 --> 01:14:58,320
what is hard and what they want teachers
to understand.

1094
01:14:58,320 --> 01:15:01,840
Taking their views seriously supports
self-advocacy,

1095
01:15:01,840 --> 01:15:05,280
including when their answer is different
from what adults expected.

1096
01:15:07,320 --> 01:15:10,480
In Session One, we looked at your rights
at school.

1097
01:15:10,480 --> 01:15:12,640
Under the Disability Standards for
Education,

1098
01:15:12,640 --> 01:15:14,600
the school must consult your young person,

1099
01:15:14,600 --> 01:15:18,360
or you on their behalf, about reasonable
adjustments.

1100
01:15:18,360 --> 01:15:21,760
This checklist helps make that
consultation useful.

1101
01:15:21,760 --> 01:15:23,400
Consultation is more useful

1102
01:15:23,400 --> 01:15:25,920
when there is a clear shared purpose,

1103
01:15:25,920 --> 01:15:29,680
open questions and more than one way to
contribute.

1104
01:15:29,680 --> 01:15:31,720
Your knowledge and your young person's
knowledge

1105
01:15:31,720 --> 01:15:33,000
deserve attention.

1106
01:15:33,000 --> 01:15:36,240
What was heard needs to be recorded
accurately,

1107
01:15:36,240 --> 01:15:39,760
followed by an agreed action,
responsibility and follow-up.

1108
01:15:39,760 --> 01:15:43,840
The checklist on page sixteen can help
before and after a meeting.

1109
01:15:43,840 --> 01:15:45,960
Ask, listen and follow up.

1110
01:15:45,960 --> 01:15:48,680
Before leaving, check who will do what

1111
01:15:48,680 --> 01:15:51,360
and when you will hear how it is going.

1112
01:15:51,360 --> 01:15:53,200
A conversation becomes more useful

1113
01:15:53,200 --> 01:15:55,680
when everyone understands the next step.

1114
01:15:57,480 --> 01:16:00,380
Secondary schools have more people and
systems to navigate.

1115
01:16:00,380 --> 01:16:06,540
A home-room teacher, pastoral care teacher
or year coordinator may help

1116
01:16:06,540 --> 01:16:07,860
with everyday matters.

1117
01:16:07,860 --> 01:16:12,600
Learning support or an inclusion team may
coordinate adjustments.

1118
01:16:12,600 --> 01:16:16,820
Wellbeing staff or a school counsellor may
support wellbeing.

1119
01:16:16,820 --> 01:16:19,920
Names and responsibilities differ between
schools.

1120
01:16:19,920 --> 01:16:23,320
On page sixteen, note who you would
contact and how.

1121
01:16:23,320 --> 01:16:26,340
If you're unsure, mark the question to ask
school.

1122
01:16:26,940 --> 01:16:31,240
It can help to agree one main contact, and
learn the portal or app

1123
01:16:31,240 --> 01:16:32,320
alongside your young person.

1124
01:16:32,320 --> 01:16:35,240
Pause while you make a start.

1125
01:16:43,680 --> 01:16:46,420
You don't have to complete every name now.

1126
01:16:46,420 --> 01:16:49,020
Start with the person who knows your young
person well

1127
01:16:49,020 --> 01:16:52,020
and ask how the school organises the rest.

1128
01:16:52,020 --> 01:16:54,860
A short written follow-up after a
conversation

1129
01:16:54,860 --> 01:16:58,100
can help everyone check what was agreed
and who will act.

1130
01:17:00,600 --> 01:17:03,640
Taking part in a meeting can happen in
different ways. Your young person

1131
01:17:03,640 --> 01:17:05,600
might attend, write something beforehand,

1132
01:17:05,600 --> 01:17:10,680
use their communication tools or choose a
trusted adult to share their

1133
01:17:10,680 --> 01:17:11,600
views.

1134
01:17:11,600 --> 01:17:14,870
Preparation matters. Ask what they want
said, what they'd like to say

1135
01:17:14,870 --> 01:17:18,600
themselves and how they can signal that
they need a break.

1136
01:17:18,600 --> 01:17:22,980
Participation can grow over time,
including choosing their own agenda

1137
01:17:22,980 --> 01:17:23,600
items.

1138
01:17:23,600 --> 01:17:27,390
It should be supported and chosen. If they
don't want to attend, their

1139
01:17:27,390 --> 01:17:29,600
perspective can still be included.

1140
01:17:29,600 --> 01:17:33,600
If they say no to a support, that view
belongs in the discussion too.

1141
01:17:33,600 --> 01:17:37,600
This is practice in having a say about
their learning and their life.

1142
01:17:40,240 --> 01:17:43,800
The Working Together Communication Tool,
on the left, helps you agree how

1143
01:17:43,800 --> 01:17:44,640
home and school

1144
01:17:44,640 --> 01:17:46,440
will communicate.

1145
01:17:46,440 --> 01:17:51,190
You can choose a method, such as email,
phone or a communication book,

1146
01:17:51,190 --> 01:17:52,600
then record how often,

1147
01:17:52,600 --> 01:17:56,520
what you'll communicate about, and the
next dates or steps.

1148
01:17:56,520 --> 01:18:00,680
The Partnership Planning Template, on the
right, asks what has worked,

1149
01:18:00,680 --> 01:18:02,040
what has been challenging

1150
01:18:02,040 --> 01:18:04,160
and what could help.

1151
01:18:04,160 --> 01:18:08,120
You can use those prompts before a meeting
or complete them together.

1152
01:18:08,120 --> 01:18:11,120
A tool gives the conversation a place to
start.

1153
01:18:11,120 --> 01:18:14,600
Keep listening and your young person's own
communication alongside it.

1154
01:18:17,040 --> 01:18:22,680
The N C C D Meetings Tool Kit is a free
resource for families and schools.

1155
01:18:22,680 --> 01:18:26,160
It can help clarify the purpose of a
meeting about adjustments,

1156
01:18:26,160 --> 01:18:29,840
what information to bring, how
consultation will happen

1157
01:18:29,840 --> 01:18:32,800
and what needs to happen afterwards.

1158
01:18:32,800 --> 01:18:35,360
The link is at the back of your workbook.

1159
01:18:35,360 --> 01:18:37,640
Schools follow their own system's
processes,

1160
01:18:37,640 --> 01:18:42,240
so ask who is responsible for each step
and how follow-up will happen.

1161
01:18:42,240 --> 01:18:45,240
The tool kit gives you a way to prepare
questions and understand what to

1162
01:18:45,240 --> 01:18:46,280
expect before,

1163
01:18:46,280 --> 01:18:47,720
during and after the meeting.

1164
01:18:49,640 --> 01:18:51,520
There are supports beyond this workshop.

1165
01:18:51,520 --> 01:18:55,200
Autism Connect provides autism information
and support.

1166
01:18:55,200 --> 01:18:59,380
Disability Gateway helps people find
disability information and

1167
01:18:59,380 --> 01:18:59,980
services.

1168
01:18:59,980 --> 01:19:02,480
Carer Gateway supports carers.

1169
01:19:02,480 --> 01:19:05,720
The contact details are at the back of
your workbook.

1170
01:19:05,720 --> 01:19:10,740
For young people, Kids Helpline offers
counselling for ages five to twenty-five
on

1171
01:19:10,740 --> 01:19:14,520
eighteen hundred, fifty-five, eighteen
hundred.

1172
01:19:15,400 --> 01:19:19,850
Its website also has parent information
and directs adult parents to

1173
01:19:19,850 --> 01:19:21,900
Parentline for parenting counselling.

1174
01:19:21,900 --> 01:19:27,210
Headspace offers support for ages twelve
to twenty-five, with information for
family

1175
01:19:27,210 --> 01:19:28,120
and friends too.

1176
01:19:28,120 --> 01:19:32,580
Lifeline's crisis support number is
thirteen, eleven, fourteen.

1177
01:19:32,580 --> 01:19:35,660
Take a moment to note the services
relevant to you.

1178
01:19:35,660 --> 01:19:39,660
If you need support, reaching out is a
practical next step.

1179
01:19:45,360 --> 01:19:49,040
You don't need to know every answer about
life after school.

1180
01:19:49,040 --> 01:19:53,360
Starting conversations in the middle years
allows more time to explore

1181
01:19:53,360 --> 01:19:54,320
interests,

1182
01:19:54,320 --> 01:19:57,440
subject choices and possible pathways.

1183
01:19:57,440 --> 01:20:00,080
Include your young person at every step.

1184
01:20:00,080 --> 01:20:03,680
Options can include further study,
training, work,

1185
01:20:03,680 --> 01:20:07,040
supported employment and community
participation.

1186
01:20:07,040 --> 01:20:10,800
Different support needs deserve equally
thoughtful planning.

1187
01:20:10,800 --> 01:20:14,720
Careers staff, learning support and
relevant disability or employment

1188
01:20:14,720 --> 01:20:15,280
services

1189
01:20:15,280 --> 01:20:18,160
can help clarify what is available.

1190
01:20:18,160 --> 01:20:19,760
Keep questions open:

1191
01:20:19,760 --> 01:20:21,760
what matters to your young person,

1192
01:20:21,760 --> 01:20:25,560
what would they like to explore, and what
support would make that

1193
01:20:25,560 --> 01:20:26,320
possible?

1194
01:20:26,320 --> 01:20:30,230
An interest can offer a starting point
without becoming a decision made

1195
01:20:30,230 --> 01:20:31,360
for them.

1196
01:20:33,040 --> 01:20:37,360
Turn to page seventeen and choose one
purposeful conversation with school.

1197
01:20:37,360 --> 01:20:39,360
What needs to be understood?

1198
01:20:39,360 --> 01:20:42,520
What question could open the conversation?

1199
01:20:42,520 --> 01:20:47,180
How will your young person take part, or
have their views included?

1200
01:20:47,180 --> 01:20:50,400
What could be decided together, and how
will you follow up?

1201
01:20:50,400 --> 01:20:51,860
Use short notes.

1202
01:20:51,860 --> 01:20:55,140
Bring in one idea from the power activity
that could make the

1203
01:20:55,140 --> 01:20:56,520
conversation more balanced.

1204
01:20:56,520 --> 01:20:59,600
There is no need to share a sensitive
situation.

1205
01:21:00,000 --> 01:21:02,730
Plan to talk with your young person about
the conversation before it

1206
01:21:02,730 --> 01:21:03,300
happens.

1207
01:21:03,300 --> 01:21:05,600
Allow six minutes to prepare.

1208
01:21:05,600 --> 01:21:09,480
Pause the video here, and resume when you
are ready to return.

1209
01:21:17,960 --> 01:21:21,400
Circle the question you want to open with.

1210
01:21:21,400 --> 01:21:24,360
Keep these notes for Session Six.

1211
01:21:24,360 --> 01:21:27,120
A small, specific next step is enough:

1212
01:21:27,120 --> 01:21:29,280
one thing that needs to be understood,

1213
01:21:29,280 --> 01:21:31,760
and a way to hear from your young person
as part of it.

1214
01:21:38,360 --> 01:21:40,520
We've reached Full Circle.

1215
01:21:40,520 --> 01:21:43,160
Look back at page four, at the young
person you described

1216
01:21:43,160 --> 01:21:45,400
and the one thing you wanted to know.

1217
01:21:45,400 --> 01:21:47,280
What feels clearer now?

1218
01:21:47,280 --> 01:21:48,600
What question remains?

1219
01:21:48,600 --> 01:21:52,560
Take a moment privately, or share a brief
reflection

1220
01:21:52,560 --> 01:21:53,400
if you wish.

1221
01:21:53,400 --> 01:21:58,000
Then look again at the strengths and joys
you wrote down.

1222
01:21:58,000 --> 01:22:00,520
Pause here before we choose what to carry
forward.

1223
01:22:09,360 --> 01:22:11,800
Keep that whole person in view.

1224
01:22:11,800 --> 01:22:15,450
We're going to finish with a few small
actions that connect home,

1225
01:22:15,450 --> 01:22:17,800
school and positive identity.

1226
01:22:17,800 --> 01:22:20,710
You don't need a long list, and you don't
need to have answered every

1227
01:22:20,710 --> 01:22:21,440
question today.

1228
01:22:24,040 --> 01:22:28,440
Belonging, capacity and joy give us three
connected questions to return

1229
01:22:28,440 --> 01:22:29,040
to.

1230
01:22:29,040 --> 01:22:33,540
Can your young person be recognised,
included and able to participate

1231
01:22:33,540 --> 01:22:35,040
as themselves?

1232
01:22:35,040 --> 01:22:37,040
What does the day cost,

1233
01:22:37,040 --> 01:22:40,040
and what protects or restores energy?

1234
01:22:40,040 --> 01:22:44,040
Where are the interests, connections and
moments of authentic enjoyment?

1235
01:22:44,040 --> 01:22:49,040
When you try a change at home or school,
look across all three.

1236
01:22:49,040 --> 01:22:52,040
Getting through a task is only part of the
picture.

1237
01:22:52,040 --> 01:22:56,040
The effect on energy and belonging matters
too.

1238
01:22:56,040 --> 01:22:59,330
Your young person's own response will help
you judge whether a change

1239
01:22:59,330 --> 01:23:00,040
is useful.

1240
01:23:02,560 --> 01:23:04,960
Turn to Three small things on page
eighteen.

1241
01:23:04,960 --> 01:23:07,840
First, one thing to try at home this week.

1242
01:23:07,840 --> 01:23:09,620
Keep it small enough to begin.

1243
01:23:09,620 --> 01:23:14,120
Second, one thing you would like school to
know, understand or support,

1244
01:23:14,120 --> 01:23:16,340
and the sentence you will open with.

1245
01:23:16,340 --> 01:23:19,820
Your notes from page seventeen can help.

1246
01:23:19,820 --> 01:23:23,440
Third, one strength you see in your young
person,

1247
